1743:Don't be so sure.
1744:Before Yuan Xiao can stand up and come to my defense, an aloof male voice rings out from the crowd.
1745:A man dressed in cyan emerges from the crowd. He walks straight up to me, looks at my tea, then reaches over and pours it out.
1746:The art of tea ceremony can't be boiled down to a mere set of rigid rules. The water quality, temperature, and even the temperature of the cup to a certain extent will all affect the end result.
1747:As I place the brewed tea leaves into the serving pot, I catch Yuan Xiao nodding out of the corner of my eye. So far so good. So, now...
1748:Just as I'm about to serve the tea, a slender hand reaches out and snatches my teapot.
1749:I look up to see a man dressed in cyan and exuding an air of detached indifference. He studies the tea I brewed and frowns.
1750:Yep, this is the head honcho at Magical Dream--Fei Ming.
1751:You won't be able to win with this cup. Brew it again.
1752:What do you think makes a tea ceremony?
1753:Heh, you're way more honest than that slick boss of yours.
1754:Not knowing is normal. In the years since I opened my tea house, only a handful of people that really understand tea have walked through those doors. Or you could say that everyone understands, and no one understands.
1755:Sure, that's one kind of tea ceremony.
1756:Brewing directly in boiled water is a tea ceremony. Grinding it into a powder and adding boiled water to turn it into a paste is a tea ceremony. Gulping it down to quench your thirst is a tea ceremony, as is tasting it in small sips.
1757:Tea ceremony is an art of the heart. It doesn't matter what form you practice it in.
1758:So, I suggest you make a fresh pot. Don't waste the fine tea she picked out for you.
1759:Since you have time for chitchat, how about fetching your new friend here some fresh spring water? As for you... why don't you start by getting to know this tea a little.
1760:What does the scent make you think of?
1761:Oolong tea is produced in mountainous regions interlaced with rivers. So, your description is actually quite fitting.
1762:Once the tea leaves are harvested, they must be sun withered, or dried. The sun is just right for this at around four or five o'clock in the afternoon. The leaves are spread out evenly to allow the moisture to evaporate...
1763:Fei Ming's descriptions are so immersive that I feel like I'm actually experiencing the process of making tea.
1764:In a tea field halfway up the mountainside, tea pickers quickly pluck young shoots from branch after branch. Meanwhile, in a farmhouse nearby, tea leaves are being neatly arranged stack by stack...
1765:There's no hard and fast rule for making tea. The best approach is whichever suits the tea in front of you.
1766:By the time I've made a new pot of tea, everyone's been quiet for a while now. Many have their eyes fixed on the amber-colored tea before me, which fills the room with a lingering, faintly floral fragrance.
1767:Before I know it, Mr. Zhao is already in the seat in front of me. Without saying a word, he raises one of the cups of tea I've poured and takes a sip.
1768:Premium Phoenix Dan Cong Oolong. The poor climate in Nanxiang these last couple of years led to a decline in production, so you can hardly get it anywhere on Champion Street. And here you used it in the competition, hmph.
1769:I think you have praise for more than just the tea.
1770:Hmph! It's a nice thought, but there's nothing I despise more than a phony tea expert. You may have gotten lucky this time, but this is where your luck runs out.
1771:Having said his piece, Mr. Zhao raises the other cup and finishes it in a single gulp. He then departs just as swiftly.
1772:Heh. Vulgar people and high society just don't mix.
1773:The three of us were just discussing this. The fact is, each had their strengths and weaknesses. Mr. Zhao's tea was refined and consistent--perfect, even. But too much perfection is a flaw in itself. It makes the tea too inaccessible.
1774:Indeed. On the other hand, our young friend here--though slightly clumsy in technique--managed to resonate with tea and adapt to the circumstances under Fei Ming's direction. This actually brought out the greatest possible fragrance... It's hard to determine the winner.
1775:With all due respect, Magical Dream did not brew their tea using the proper methods. How could it be a draw?
1776:That's not entirely accurate, San Niang. Of the two competitors from Magical Dream, one pursued an ancient technique and the other engaged in a dance of sorts with the tea. It serves to remind us just what the art of tea brewing is.
1777:It's either immutable and cautious or showcases the tea leaves' true charm. The three of us subscribe to the latter interpretation.
1778:Paying no further attention to San Niang, the judges--waxing poetic and exchanging impassioned interjections--walk off together.
1779:San Niang glares fiercely at me through the crowd. She then grabs hold of Mr. Li--who's been slumped over dejectedly ever since they lost the first round--and drags him out of the tea house with her.
1780:See, what did I tell you? You have a grudge being held against you now.
1781:Yep, you won.
1782:I guess that makes me something of a hero then.
1783:To my surprise, Fei Ming doesn't take it. Instead, he waves it away, tosses a seal stamp to Yuan Xiao, and heads up to the third floor.
1784:Still, Mr. Fei Ming is much easier to talk to than I imagined.
1785:Secondly, as a businesswoman, Fuuka Minami is an expert in cost-benefit analysis. Were this tea competition mishandled, both parties would take offense, and she'd most certainly find an excuse to wash her hands off the whole affair--naturally leaving the proposed partnership with Fei Ming dead in the water.
1786:So, my friend... you really were the hero of the day in every respect.
1787:Hehe, aw shucks, all these compliments are embarrassing me. It's fine though, you can keep going. I can take it.
1788:The truth is, this should have been an uneventful matter, but your involvement breathed life back into it. It's quite interesting, really. I think Fei Ming realized that as well, which is why he agreed to cooperate with you.
1789:Okay. I've had a quick look at the contract, and Fei Ming will decide which tea to supply. He'll hash out the other details with your boss.
1790:Now all you have to do is go back, enjoy a well-deserved rest, and pick up the tea samples tomorrow so you can report back.
1791:No, silly! We're ALL done with work for the day! Hurray for clocking out! Hurray for no more tea competition!
